{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152158 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a18b37e2-4ab1-5fd1-b985-3021172abc41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6306" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2} \\, \\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y = x$ for $\\, 0 \\le x \\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}({4}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542605 :question-generator/id #uuid "c11006f1-1a4a-5211-b8df-123912d95ea1" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186152158.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f6daa38-7471-5f48-a645-2a58b0ccbb8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342894} {:answer/id #uuid "4f78e0c3-9035-5dc2-8ab5-f198ada876f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342898} {:answer/id #uuid "57ea0380-1042-5b37-9a3a-4b0d0e5dc158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342971} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d08ff9-8579-514b-818e-d1904f4ed1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343798} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c6af53-8fe9-5d44-852a-2dfdb428e5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c6af53-8fe9-5d44-852a-2dfdb428e5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344257} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a857e8-a364-5f7f-9a5f-59f962b7904c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78a90830-8528-5d8e-9b99-56c7720a064a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76483" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c03a658-3ced-5c53-8935-8cd4c7daaee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150984} {:answer/id #uuid "92aaba17-f7e1-5c37-9c23-985c2dd63e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150985} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5aa0ec-6082-55e3-9533-1691c19eb313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150986} {:answer/id #uuid "1df132c5-ce6b-50c6-af6a-10e7041781c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150987} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1e765a-764a-5dc0-a4c7-3a07dea17ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150988}] :answer-field/id #uuid "686dc23d-0f1e-5e4d-9da1-a23da84b778b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150983}] :question/id #uuid "cc7e2b92-479b-55b5-8bf7-4a42b47fa43c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76508" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = x(y - 1)$ along the line $y = 2x + 1$, $0 \\le x \\le 1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1789b93e-e90d-51e6-86cd-985dcd4c2ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272592} {:answer/id #uuid "7e646a2c-1e40-5435-9dcf-64c1e220e31c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273469} {:answer/id #uuid "93a7c64c-5182-542e-b0fc-91eb0d61f60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273673} {:answer/id #uuid "c7199719-0890-571a-9338-3d297facad42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274116} {:answer/id #uuid "cdab7799-003c-5d60-89a7-3513310a8ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdab7799-003c-5d60-89a7-3513310a8ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274177} :answer-field/id #uuid "b59ab03d-3465-5b29-9e7b-a4f6a51317aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42825c99-640f-50c0-a09a-bfc117ceeb27" :question/math-academy-id "q-148215" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2xy$ along the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2t\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0008a7ab-2b00-5ba5-82d0-3f2e6b8bdaa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272388} {:answer/id #uuid "17e48ee3-60bc-5378-96bd-24895594a042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272596} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec1aa77-4fa6-5d21-87e6-58f157cb48ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273050} {:answer/id #uuid "51233414-b60c-52f0-9294-6615ffa1a531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273078} {:answer/id #uuid "969f01e3-5935-5175-9bab-003ea3ffc27f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0008a7ab-2b00-5ba5-82d0-3f2e6b8bdaa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272388} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad4ad789-849d-5b1e-a803-15e978d32631" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "433c5408-32dd-5664-8e15-0cc00f55a35f" :question/math-academy-id "q-148216" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the line $y=x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t+t=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({3}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34291103-4a5e-5f9d-8144-2820b64dc232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272851} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1ac0a8-d747-5642-84bf-d3bedb0860b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272901} {:answer/id #uuid "41fc5647-a796-5301-9030-d502b60a9abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272948} {:answer/id #uuid "d25ab67d-86b3-5cbb-9152-9babdc82e835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274227} {:answer/id #uuid "db13e736-05fd-5a35-b7d3-84b47c65b56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db13e736-05fd-5a35-b7d3-84b47c65b56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274303} :answer-field/id #uuid "906d2ea1-bc54-585c-a9b0-7daff68f61ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43f73019-6edc-5de1-83b9-c96af73e24f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-148218" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,3t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)j \\\\  & =i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\,(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dec25eb-c4e4-5dd8-8858-a2052e0e7cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273192} {:answer/id #uuid "941af366-6fdf-509f-b872-9d3472fbbf0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273675} {:answer/id #uuid "9e572a0b-0165-5642-9463-9a75cb4f7d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273754} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd85e82-26c3-57a0-b7b4-34dcbb7eb698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274169} {:answer/id #uuid "f43d85f4-5fca-5864-b495-5e0b60a2529d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "941af366-6fdf-509f-b872-9d3472fbbf0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273675} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c951af-26dd-5fa2-96f0-6a3db91ee272" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a58d761-ea89-5d38-aaa2-f03aa1143709" :question/math-academy-id "q-148219" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-y}$ along the line $y=x-4,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=t-4.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)j \\\\  & =i+\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(t-4))}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\,t\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0470023d-2f7c-572b-8223-a821e9591b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286} {:answer/id #uuid "21e4ce2b-359a-5d4a-b474-cf8acda842ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342519} {:answer/id #uuid "3109d7d4-9f83-52e3-8ef2-13bdd11c2459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342650} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d1ba27-795d-5ca7-bff5-0a3d75d5b66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343667} {:answer/id #uuid "d10bbab9-4de9-5d18-a90a-7d251ca7ad2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0470023d-2f7c-572b-8223-a821e9591b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec0e9-f2ac-56af-9dc2-ed440ea409ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2497006d-58cf-53a4-9f8c-593f6d881048" :question/math-academy-id "q-76431" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 4\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{t}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})}{t}=\\frac{t}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{t}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{25}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}{|}_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({25}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(125-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d872230-dffa-5440-9150-e6a7484563c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342745} {:answer/id #uuid "41027de0-afa8-5c2b-8896-f3199a916c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342773} {:answer/id #uuid "679fdec6-f7e1-5541-a399-fb14f4197dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120} {:answer/id #uuid "970fe583-040b-5004-90dd-58e93faa89d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(16\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343562} {:answer/id #uuid "bad2408b-9fca-5a3a-8105-a9d53cef466b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679fdec6-f7e1-5541-a399-fb14f4197dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5d8aa7-e05a-504e-8119-e3b3b13e8c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d9906db-a501-54d2-bbd1-292e69086032" :question/math-academy-id "q-76480" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $\\,1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}3{x}^{5}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}$, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{3/2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8160d239-5719-5314-9b34-25360242df8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542577} {:answer/id #uuid "9618b0be-10e0-54af-bddc-144790bcb25d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542583} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa66a-4ae8-5eb8-a19d-10e8556f0769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542588} {:answer/id #uuid "c98eda7f-7904-59d5-aed5-f095f55995d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542592} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f6bd32-8ed5-5aec-826d-0d30d73baf91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f6bd32-8ed5-5aec-826d-0d30d73baf91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542594} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a59230-cdc0-59cf-8d49-a7d059d179cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2564c8e6-dd58-5512-bb79-d13a805cc112" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x$ along the line $y=2 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(t)(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{\\sqrt{5} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left((3)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left(9-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020d5907-9b7b-5719-a1f1-4f44c5409284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542557} {:answer/id #uuid "33be0b46-f243-554c-9d9e-964dc5eb30b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542564} {:answer/id #uuid "6c57458e-2426-55cb-b5d8-65aeae4d3130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542574} {:answer/id #uuid "c56967e6-dc2a-5dcc-9976-37549a83e6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542591} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e63c2b-a619-54eb-9772-8aa7f8b9dbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "020d5907-9b7b-5719-a1f1-4f44c5409284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542557} :answer-field/id #uuid "d538b1dd-c792-572f-8c1f-8353d9802950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bf9d63c-b3f4-5d38-83c5-c18e8598dd9c" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$ along the line $y=2 x + 1,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t + 1.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t + 1)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}(2 t^{2})(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5} t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left((2)^{3}-(0)^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left(8-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07329d51-3f5e-5e2e-8441-b2141aff317e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35 \\sqrt{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542558} {:answer/id #uuid "224fc2fd-156e-5950-be40-d81695a7789b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542562} {:answer/id #uuid "38a9bc69-d00d-5b76-b39d-357b71605439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542565} {:answer/id #uuid "7105e2b6-33c5-596d-aaf1-e33906b7e9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542575} {:answer/id #uuid "f9544065-9a17-5e0c-a38b-236361697c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9065}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a9bc69-d00d-5b76-b39d-357b71605439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542565} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f81595a-0aec-5788-9a31-df107d898cd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eade040-7c90-5638-966c-718b41cfb998" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=5 x$ along the line $y=6 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 7.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=6 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,6 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 7\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(6 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(6)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(6)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{37}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{7}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{7}(5 t)(\\sqrt{37})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{7} \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left((7)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left(49-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e40f44d-615f-5865-a602-f53e98d24e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15 \\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542561} {:answer/id #uuid "65d7c7ed-cf67-5d74-9d2d-7d230e621f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1275}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542572} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e57f6d-ff24-5498-8177-a14094d5bd2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542586} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc6fca7-a70d-5ad4-b81d-03df2ea33f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542589} {:answer/id #uuid "cc301a85-6415-5f5a-a1ec-c9814b9c754e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e57f6d-ff24-5498-8177-a14094d5bd2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542586} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b9afa8e-4db7-5a3a-a384-3177ceb89729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab95a8c-ac51-5396-919b-6a451ca13ac6" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 x$ along the line $y=4 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 5.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=4 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,4 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(4 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(4)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(4)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{5}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{5}(3 t)(\\sqrt{17})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left((5)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left(25-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b718af0-92d3-59c9-9229-69b2816bd3cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542559} {:answer/id #uuid "53bf3405-0c98-553b-9ce6-af3d6065c7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542568} {:answer/id #uuid "5c916508-666a-5d4f-bc0e-83237918f953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{380}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542569} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd61603-c8b6-5e98-acdd-11a2306975c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542571} {:answer/id #uuid "9a892440-907b-5477-a7e0-478061c8676f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53bf3405-0c98-553b-9ce6-af3d6065c7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542568} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c75bfe-126d-5238-b88a-6cc39af08281" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74138378-8762-53b8-ac46-3f0ace177732" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,3t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)j \\\\  & =i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\,(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{8}{3}+6+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2c9bda-2b1c-547d-a80e-eff23e39c06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542563} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9920b7-0e21-5c1f-8111-86f228f479e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542580} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2807fa-9092-5089-8280-849eef5d1d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{285}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542581} {:answer/id #uuid "9e06ccd9-0287-543d-9f3b-377e3c903179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(18446744073709551616 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542585} {:answer/id #uuid "bc780127-f10d-5f5e-8853-c61ea96f5041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3} + \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c2c9bda-2b1c-547d-a80e-eff23e39c06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542563} :answer-field/id #uuid "927562b5-720d-53ed-bf92-54fcb887de44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e94a1c8-32a5-5aeb-82a3-988f3971786f" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{17}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({17}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(17 \\sqrt{17}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482b716c-5732-5534-84fd-923827a99c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542566} {:answer/id #uuid "6a75de59-ff24-5015-82fd-549b0eabf5f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 72 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542573} {:answer/id #uuid "77c7a369-2abc-53dd-b2aa-5a817fb5b5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542576} {:answer/id #uuid "871f3101-6660-58fb-8c35-50aad648b843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542579} {:answer/id #uuid "8d989555-4656-5801-a461-47d42c1dd876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77c7a369-2abc-53dd-b2aa-5a817fb5b5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542576} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a0cf1d-2ab0-543e-9e64-7b0edf5e8453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "969d5e52-a658-5dd3-9654-fc884ce1b67b" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=4 x$ along the line $y=5 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 6.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,5 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(5 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(5)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(5)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=4 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{6}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{6}(4 t)(\\sqrt{26})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[2 \\sqrt{26} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{6} \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{26}\\left((6)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{26}\\left(36-0\\right) \\\\ &=72 \\sqrt{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d630ca6-fd28-58ed-995a-8da968fd5ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542560} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0613bc-32a4-53a2-a943-9e50b992e369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542567} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa57f44-2642-593c-a3e7-ef6c93fe59eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542570} {:answer/id #uuid "867db4e2-70dd-5f07-8dc4-522fdb1b0abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542578} {:answer/id #uuid "b4af4815-a8ff-5192-b441-27ae750e4043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0613bc-32a4-53a2-a943-9e50b992e369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542567} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7d77a5-ca55-5835-beb8-b8509e3fef56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa2eae58-23f0-53d8-954b-616c559e82a9" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x$ along the line $y=3 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,3 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(3 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(3)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{4}(2 t)(\\sqrt{10})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\sqrt{10} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{4} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left((4)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left(16-0\\right) \\\\ &=16 \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}
